# ABSTRACT

A simple and green approach was developed to extract the pepsin soluble collagen-II using the ultra-sonication treatment that significantly increased the extracted yield from chicken sternal cartilage (CSC). The pretreatment of raw CSC had positive effects on proximate composition. The maximum yield of pepsin soluble collagen was obtained by ultrasound treatment time 36 minutes (UPSCI136; 3.37 g) as compared to non-ultrasound treated pepsin soluble collagen at zero minutes (UPSCI10; 1.73 g) (control). The amino acid profile, differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) of UPSC were significantly \((\mathsf{p}<0.05)\) improved by the application of ultrasound. The results showed the secondary structure of ultrasound treated PSC-II was partially altered as the ultra-sonication time prolonged. Moreover, ultrasound-treated collagen had superior functional properties such as water, oil absorption capacity, water holding capacity, foaming and emulsifying properties than non-ultrasound treated collagen. The poultry by-products CSC would be a potential source of land animal collagen-II. The utilization of ultrasound for the extraction of pepsin soluble collagen-II is a good alternative technology to expand the application of collagen at industrial level.

Keywords: By-product; ultrasonic; enzyme; collagen; physicochemical; functional characteristics

# 1. Introduction:

Meat industry is the domain of food industry which produces tons of wastes (by-products) such as bones (hard & soft), tendons, blood, internal organs and skin during slaughtering (Jayathilakan, Sultana, Radhakrishna, & Bawa, 2012). These wastes are non-edible portion of meat and not useful in processed meat products. Generally, these by-products disappear from local markets and find place in slums generating pollution and other health concerns for human
populations. Efficient utilization of by-products plays important role in the economy as well as impacting directly on environmental pollution. In the previous study (Shen et al, 2019) the chondroitin sulfate was extracted from chicken cartilage by the steam explosion membrane separation advance technique. The chondroitin sulfate have potential to be used as dietary supplements for the treatment of osteoarthritis.

These problems in advanced growing populations urges meat scientists and processors to pay attention for the reprocessing of such commodities by dedicated research efforts (Cárcel, Benedito, Bon, & Mulet, 2007). In many cases, meat, poultry, and fish processing by-product wastes have the potential for valuable utilization by processing into highly valuable products (Jeevithan, Jingyi, Wang, He, Bao, & Wu, 2015).The growth of poultry processing industries efficiently depends upon waste product management.

The poultry processing is the fastest growing meat sector, due to rapid change in trends of food culture especially in processed meat products where bone-free meat is consumer's preference (Chang, Xu, Zhou, Li, & Huang, 2012). The soft bone is the major by-product of meat processing industries and for the extraction of collagens can be used as raw material. The collagen is a most important structural protein in animal bodies comprising approximately \(30\%\) of total protein. At least 29 different types of collagens have been identified which vary in their sequence and composition of amino acid. Based on their location and functions in the body, collagens can be categorized into three groups fibril, network forming and fibril-associated (Gómez-Guillén et al., 2011; Liu, Liang, Regenstein, & Zhou, 2012). In all these types, collagen type I and II are most abundantly found in mammals.

Advanced emerging technologies are required as an alternative to conventional processes. Among the emerging technologies, high pressure, electrical pulses, microfiltration, and ultra-
sonication are included. They all are specially designed as economical, time saving and effective (Chemat & Ashokkumar, 2017).

By ultrasound, a set of mechanical waves composed a physical environment that alters the physical and functional properties (Pakbin, Rezaei, & Haghighi, 2015; Povey & Mason, 1998). In a liquid, the effect of ultrasound waves is based on the cavitation, the mechanism of the formation, growth, and collapse of small bubbles. These bubbles in the liquid medium over several cycles can grow larger by absorbing the gas or vapor, formed then collapse in the second stage of the cavitation cycle to release the absorbed energy and to give rise to such mechanical effects as local heat and high pressure (Amiri, Sharifian, & Soltanizadeh, 2018; Chemat & Khan, 2011). The resulting stresses and the repeated collapse of the bubbles bring about rupturing the walls and surfaces of the particles (Piyasena, Mohareb, & McKellar, 2003). Ultrasound waves are used to enhance the different characteristics of raw material (Alarcon-Rojo et al., 2015). In ultrasound, electrical energy is converted into mechanical energy in the form of vibration energy that produce cavitation effect on the raw material altering its physical, chemical and biological properties (Demirdöven & Baysal, 2008).

Furthermore, in comparison to conventional methods, ultra-sonication technique is non-invasive and nondestructive (Jayasooriya, Bhandari, Torley, & D'arcy, 2004). By the use of ultrasound treatment physicochemical characteristics, functional properties of protein were improved due to the cavitation force of ultrasound waves (Akram & Zhang, 2020; Amiri, Sharifian, & Soltanizadeh, 2018). Previous studies exhibited the high power ultrasound have potential to activate the immobilized enzymes through increasing rate of transport of substrates to enzymes (Cárcel, Benedito, Bon, & Mulet, 2007).
It is an effective, easy to use in the laboratories and in the industries for production lines fast and automated. The use of ultrasound in research is to analyze the desirable and undesirable degradation of the material. This technique is important in the meat sector for upscaling ultrasonic technology (Chemat & Ashokkumar, 2017). Previous research findings propose that application of ultrasound with probe had a significant outcome on extraction of collagen contents as well as in modifying the characteristics such as thermal stability, structural and functional properties of the protein (collagen) (Chang, Xu, Zhou, Li, & Huang, 2012).

However, in the last few years, extraction of collagen was ignored due to religious and cultural reasons. The main sources of collagen from land animals such as pigs, cows and poultry were hardly employed for by-products processing (Magnusson et al., 2018). Collagen usually extracted from the processing of marine wastes (by-products) skin, bones and swim bladders etc. is utilized as an alternative source for the collagen production (Abedin et al., 2013; Zhang et al., 2010). The previous studies have been stated the physicochemical and functional properties of pepsin soluble collagen from poultry. In the present study, soft bone (chicken sternal cartilage) was utilized as raw material for the extraction of collagen-II, not just to overcome the environmental pollution but also to produce a value-added products for potential application in different processing industries. Besides, it also aims to discussing in the advanced and effective method for extraction of pepsin soluble collagen. Ultrasound application will contribute to the extraction of pepsin soluble collagen under the appropriate conditions from chicken sternal cartilage and present significant effect on physicochemical, biochemical and functional properties, which remains unreported.

# 2. Materials and methods

# 2.1. Raw material and chemicals
Raw material chicken sternal cartilage from meat processing industry Doyoo (Henan) Industrial Co., Ltd. China. The sample was stored at \(-20^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The pepsin was purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (USA). All other chemicals used in the experiments such as acetic acid, sodium chloride, hydroxyproline standard all chemicals were used analytical grade.

# 2.2. Pretreatment and sample preparation

The frozen soft bone chicken sternal cartilage (CSC) was first thawed at \(4{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), washed with distilled water. To get rid of non-collagenous proteins, fat and pigments, the pretreatment was performed by previously describe method of Jeevithan et al., (2015) with slight modifications. The cartilage pieces added into the solution contain \(20\;\%\), w/v NaCl, 0.05 M Tris HCl maintained pH 7.5, at ratio \(1{:}10\ \mathrm{w/v}\), and overnight stirring on magnetic stirrer then washed by distilled water to remove fats and bubbles. To the removal of pigments \(0.5\;\mathrm{M}\;\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}\) at ratio of \(1{:}10\ \mathrm{w/v}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\). Then \(0.3\;\mathrm{M}\) EDTA- \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\) at a ratio of \(1{:}10\ \mathrm{w/v}\), \(\mathrm{pH}\,7.4\) to de-mineralize the cartilage pieces for \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\). The solutions were renewed after every \(8\,\mathrm{h}\). The pieces were washed with distilled water until \(\mathrm{pH}\,7.0\). The pretreated CSC pieces blended by using a blending machine (Tate Apparatus Co., Ltd. Tianjin, China), stored at \(-20\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) until further use.

# 2.3. Extraction methods

# 2.3.1. Non-ultrasound-treated pepsin soluble collagen-II

The conventional extraction of pepsin soluble collagen-II was termed as (control) non-ultrasonic treated pepsin soluble collagen-II (UPSCIII0) (zero min of ultrasound treatment). The extraction of collagen from CSC was applied as previously described (Khong et al., 2018). Briefly, the pretreated CSC was suspended into \(0.5\;\mathrm{M}\) acetic acid \(1{:}6\ \mathrm{w/v}\). The \(\mathrm{pH}\,2.8\) was maintained with formic acid then \(0.1\;\%\) pepsin was added, mixed it thoroughly and put on the magnetic stirrer for stirring \(96\mathrm{h}\) at \(4\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Sample was centrifuged (Himac, CR21N, Hitachi Co.,
Tokyo, Japan) at \(13{,}000\,g\) for time 1hour. Salting out procedure 1 M Tris-base was added for every liter of supernatant collected. The pH was maintained at 7.0 by adding 10 M NaOH and then NaCl powder was slowly added to the supernatant at \(5\,\mathrm{M}\) with constant stirring. The collected precipitates were centrifuge at \(13{,}000\,g\) for 1hour. The pellet was collected and then dissolved \(0.1\,\mathrm{M}\) acetic acid. The sample was dialyzed (Dialysis bag; Mw 1200-1400, MD44-5M, MYM, USA) against distilled water at \(4{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and then lyophilized. The lyophilized pepsin soluble collagen-II was stored at \(-20\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) until further use.

2.3.2. Ultrasound-treated pepsin soluble collagen-II
The extraction of pepsin soluble collagen-II with ultrasound was performed using, an ultrasonic homogenizer with a probe (Ningbo Xinzhi Biotechnology Co., Ltd. Scientz-IID,

![]('img_url')

China). The pretreated CSC sample was suspended in \(0.5\,\mathrm{M}\) acetic acid \(1:6\,\mathrm{w/v}\), mixed it

thoroughly. The sample volume \(300\,\mathrm{ml}\) was subjected to ultrasonic homogenizer a maximum output power 950W, frequency \(20{-}25\,\mathrm{kHz}\) with amplitude X 10. The sample was sonicated in glass beaker equipped with the ice jacket. The treatment times were 6, 12, 24 and 36 minutes and ultrasound pulsed mode of on-time 2 s and off-time 3 s was used, which termed as UPSCII6, UPSCII12, UPSCII24, and UPSCII36, respectively. During ultrasound treatment the high-grade titanium alloy probe, diameter 6mm was immersed in the sample solution to a depth of \(3\,\mathrm{cm}\) from the bottom. After ultrasound treatment, maintain the solution \(\mathrm{pH}\,2.8\) with formic acid then put the ultrasound treated sample on a magnetic stirrer for 96h at \(4{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Stirring speed was constant for all ultrasound treated samples. The next steps were performed as mentioned in section 2.3.1.
