# 2.4. Determination of ultrasound power and intensity:

An ultrasound power is mechanical energy that is partially lost in the form of heat when ultrasound passes through medium and the waves produce heat in the liquid medium. The dissipated output power (acoustic power) in the liquid was calculated Raso et al (1999). The temperature of the solvent increase in defined processing time 120 s leads to the acoustic power calculation in Watt (W) by following equation.

\[
\begin{array}{r}\mathrm{P}=\mathrm{m}\times\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{P}}\times\frac{\mathrm{d}\mathrm{T}}{\mathrm{dt}}\end{array}
\]

Where m is mass of the sonicated liquid (g), \(C_{\mathrm{P}}\) is the specific heat capacity of the solvent (J \(\mathrm{g}^{-1}\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}^{-1},\) ).The ultrasound power intensity is expressed in watts per unit of emitting surface (Wcm2) or in watts per unit volume of the sonicated solution \((\mathrm{Wcm}^{-3})\) (Jambrak et al., 2009). In this experiment, the ultrasound intensity was \(10.692\pm0.77\;\mathrm{Wcm}^{-3}\) .

# 2.5. Proximate analysis

All the procedures were performed using the methods discussed in AOAC (2000). The protein content was determined by an automatic Kjeldahl apparatus (Kjeltec 2300, FOSS, and Denmark) with nitrogen conversion factors 6.25 for the raw material and 5.79 for lyophilized ultrasound treated collagen. Moisture (AOAC 935.29), crude fat (AOAC 920.39) and ash (AOAC 923.03) contents were measured and carbohydrate content was obtained by equation (2): Carbohydrate \(\% = 100 - \left[\text{Moisture}(\%) + \text{crude protein}(\%) + \text{fiber}(\%) + \text{crude fat}(\%) + \text{ash}(\%)\right]\)

2.6. Yield
For comparison purposes, extracted yield of collagen by non-ultrasound and ultrasound treated were calculated from 50 grams of pretreated CSC (raw material) using equations (3), (4) and (5) derived from of previous research (Khong et al., 2018).

Wet wt basis yield \((\%) = ((\text{dry wt of collagen}) / (\text{wet wt of raw material used})) \times 100 (\%)\) (3) Dry wt basis yield \((\%) = ((\text{dry wt of collagen}) / (\text{wet wt of raw material used - moisture content})) \times 100 (\%)\) (4)

Protein wt basis yield \((\%) = ((\text{dry wt of collagen}) / (\text{protein amount of raw material used})) \times 100 (\%)\) (5)

# 2.7. Gel electrophoresis

Sodium dodecyl sulfate-polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (SDS- PAGE) was performed (Laemmli's 1970) using BIO-RADMini- PROTEAN tetra system. The collagen sample \(5\;\mathrm{mg/ml}\) was dissolved in acetic acid \((0.1\;\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1})\) stirring overnight at \(4\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Then, the sample solution was centrifuged for \(20\;\mathrm{min}\) to remove undissolved particles and mixed with sample buffer at a 1:1 ratio \((\mathrm{v/v})\). The sample was heated at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(5\;\mathrm{min}\) and cooled at room temperature. The sample \(10\;\upmu\mathrm{L}\) and marker \(5\;\upmu\mathrm{L}\) were carefully loaded onto Mini-Protean-TGX-Precast Gels, run with a constant voltage for \(45\;\mathrm{min}\) at \(140\;\mathrm{V}\). The gel was stained with Coomassie brilliant blue R-250 (Mini-Protean II unit; Bio-Rad Laboratories, Inc., Hercules, CA) and put on the orbital shaker at for \(3\;\mathrm{h}\). Then destined the gel overnight with destined solution over the orbital shaker until a clear background of the gel was achieved. Ultimately SDS-PAGE gel preservative into preservation solution (7% acetic acid) until scanning by Alpha Ease FC gel imaging system (Alpha Inc., city, USA).

# 2.8. Amino acid profile
For an amino acid profile in the lyophilized collagen, sample add 6 mol/L HCl, then hydrolyzed at \(110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\). The resulting solution was measured by using HPLC gradient L8900 amino acid auto-analyzer (Hitachi Co., Tokyo, Japan).

# 2.9. UV absorption

The UV absorption spectra of sample collagen was measured using a UV spectrophotometer described by Na, (1988). The sample was dissolved in 6 M Gu-HCl, the concentration of the samples were 0.1, \(0.2\;\mathrm{mg/ml}\). The UV spectra was measured between 190-280 nm at a scan speed of \(2\;\mathrm{nm/s}\) with an interval of \(1\;\mathrm{nm}\).

# 2.10. Zeta potential and Isoelectric point (PI)

The zeta potential of collagen solution was analysed using a Zetasizer (Nano-ZS90, Malvern Instruments Ltd, United Kingdom) equipped with an avalanche photodiode detector, as previously described (Khong et al., 2018). Briefly, \(0.05\;\%\) (w/v) lyophilized ultrasound-treated pepsin soluble collagen-II was dissolved in \(0.5\;\mathrm{M}\) acetic acid. The pH values of collagen solutions \((20\;\mathrm{ml})\) were maintained at 4.0 to \(10\;\mathrm{pH}\) using \(0.25\;\mathrm{N}\) NaOH and \(0.25\;\mathrm{N}\) HCl. The PI of all samples was determined at \(\mathrm{pH}\,0.5\), then \(1\;\mathrm{mg/mL}\) was injected into clear disposable test cell (DTS1060C, Malvern Instruments Ltd. Malvern, UK) and equilibrated at \(25\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\;\mathrm{min}\) before testing. The zeta potential graph for the samples was plotted against the change of zeta potential versus the change of \(\mathrm{pH}\) and were performed in triplicate.

# 2.11. Secondary structure of collagen-II

# 2.11.1. Circular dichroism

Circular dichroism (CD) spectrum was recorded to determine the secondary structure of collagen. The lyophilized collagen \(0.5\;\mathrm{mg/ml}\) was dissolved in \(0.05\;\mathrm{M}\) acetic acid (Bio-Logic Co., Seyssinet-Pariset, France), the first reference spectrum containing acetic acid was recorded,
and then put sample solution was placed in a quartz cell with a \(1\;\mathrm{mm}\) path length. Consecutively scans were recorded at a speed of \(1000\;\mathrm{nm/min}\), wavelengths \(190{-}250\;\mathrm{nm}\), Bandwidth of \(1\;\mathrm{nm}\), response time \(0.5\;\mathrm{sec}\), and a step resolution of \(5\;\mathrm{nm}\) at \(25\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). An analysis was performed in triplicate.

# 2.11.2. Fourier Transform Infrared

FTIR spectra of lyophilized collagen samples were recorded using Nicolet 6700- Fourier transforms infrared spectrometer Canada. A spectral range \(4000{-}500\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) was analyzed, with resolution \(2\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\).

# 2.12. Thermal stability

Differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) (Model DSC822e, Mettler-Toledo GmbH, Greifensee, Switzerland) was used to measure the melting temperature (Tm) of collagen. Briefly, Sample was weighed into an aluminum pan, rehydrated with deionized water at ratio \(1.5{-}2\;\mathrm{mg}\) \(/10\upmu\mathrm{l}\) (w/v). The pan Sealed, equilibrated at \(10\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) /min and heated from \(10\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to \(100\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). An empty pan used as a reference.

# 2.13. Microscopic structure

Morphological properties of lyophilized pepsin soluble collagen were visualized by Scanning electron microscope (SEM) (S-3400n; Hitachi Ltd., Japan), after being cut into small pieces and fixed on a stub of copper with a double-sided tab. An accelerating voltage of \(15\;\mathrm{kV}\) was used to capture the image at \(\times2000\) magnification.

# 2.14. Functional properties of collagen-II

# 2.14.1. Water-holding capacity

The water-holding capacity (WHC) was calculated by previous procedure (Li, Jia, & Yao, 2009). Briefly, \(2\;\mathrm{mL}\) of distilled water and \(0.5\;\mathrm{gram}\) collagen were added into a culture dish (d
\(=10\;\mathrm{cm}\)). The dish was placed into an incubator set temperature \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with relative humidity \(75\%\). The ratio of residual water to initial water was measured by weighing the dish every 10 min for \(80\;\mathrm{min}\). The distilled water and glycerol was used as reference controls. WHC was calculated by equation (6):

WHC \((\%)=(\Delta\mathrm{W}/\mathrm{W}_{0})\times100\)

(6)

Where \(\Delta\mathrm{W}\) represents the change in the amount of water at each time point and \(\mathrm{W}_{0}\) presents the initial amount of water in the culture dish.

# 2.14.2. Water absorption capacity

The water absorption capacity (WAC) of collagen was determined using previously described method (Huang, Kuo, Wu, & Tsai, 2016). For WAC 0.5 gram of collagen was spread in the culture dish \((\mathrm{d}=10\;\mathrm{cm})\), placed in an incubator set the temperature of \(30\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with relative humidity of \(85\%\). The dish was weighed every 3 hours. The WAC was calculated by the following equations (7):

\[
\mathrm{WAC}(\%)=(\mathrm{W_t}-\mathrm{W_s})/\mathrm{W_0}\times100
\]

Where \(\mathrm{W}_{0}\) represents the weight of culture dish, \(\mathrm{W}_{\mathrm{t}}\) represents the total weight of the culture dish and \(\mathrm{W}_{\mathrm{S}}\) represents the initial sample weight.

# 2.14.3. Oil absorption capacity

Oil absorption capacity (OAC) of collagen was determined (Huang, Kuo, Wu, & Tsai, 2016). For OAC collagen 0.5 gram were placed in a centrifuge tube \(10\;\mathrm{mL}\) add soybean oil \(5\;\mathrm{mL}\) and homogenize the sample was homogenize at \(10{,}000\;\mathrm{rpm}\) (IKA \(\mathrm{T}10^{\mathrm{(\mathbb{R})}}\) basic ULTRA-TURRAX®, Germany) for \(10\;\mathrm{min}\) and stand for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) at room temperature. The mixture was centrifuged at \(10{,}000\;\mathrm{g}\) for \(25\;\mathrm{min}\). The OAC was calculated by the following equations (8):

\[
\mathrm{OAC(ml/g)}=\frac{(\mathrm{V_1-V_0})}{\mathrm{m_0}}
\]
The sample weight was defined as \(\mathbf{m}_{0}\), the volume of free oil was defined as \(\mathrm{V}_{0}\), and the initial oil was defined as \(\mathrm{V}_{1}\).

# 2.14.4. Emulsifying property

The emulsifying activity index (EAI), emulsion stability index (ESI) of extracted collagen were determined using the method reported by Pearce & Kinsella (1978). The collagen solution 6 mL 0.5% (w/v) was added to 2 mL of soybean oil and homogenized (IKA T18® digital ULTRA-TURRAX®, Germany) at a speed of 10,000 rpm for 90 s. Emulsions were diluted 100 folds with SDS 0.1% (w/v), used vortex for 10 s and absorbance at 500 nm at 0 min and 10 min using a spectrophotometer (UV-1800, Mapada Instruments Co., Ltd., Shanghai, China). EAI and ESI were calculated by equation (9) and (10):

\[
\mathrm{EAI}\left(\mathrm{m}^2/\mathrm{g}\right)=\frac{2\times2.303\times\mathrm{A}_0\times\mathrm{dilution}}{\mathrm{C}\times0.001\times\Phi\times10000}
\]

\[
\mathrm{ESI(min)}=\frac{10\times\mathrm{A_0}}{\mathrm{A_0}-\mathrm{A_{10}}}
\]

Where A0 and A10 represent the absorbance values measured at 0 min and 10 min, respectively, and C is the sample concentration \((\Phi=0.25)\).

# 2.14.5. Foaming property

The collagen foam expansion (FE) and foam stability (FS) were determined using the method reported by Shahidi et al. (1995). The collagen solution (2% w/v) was placed into a glass cylinder (100 mL). The sample solution (50 ml) was measured at pH 4, 7 and 10 and a foam was formed using a laboratory homogenizer (IKA T18® digital ULTRA-TURRAX®, Germany) at 10,000 rpm which enabled to determine the foam expansion (FE) value. The sample in which the foam expansion volume remaining after a 60 min in glass cylinder showed foam stability. FE and FS were calculated by equation (11) and (12):
\[
\begin{array}{l}\mathrm{FE(\%)}=\frac{\mathrm{V_T}}{\mathrm{V_0}}\times100\\\mathrm{FS(\%)}=\frac{\mathrm{V_t}}{\mathrm{V_0}}\times100\end{array}
\]

Where \(\mathrm{V_{T}}\) represents the total volume after whipping, \(\mathrm{V}_{0}\) represents the original volume before whipping and \(\mathrm{V_{t}}\) represents the total volume remaining after \(60\;\mathrm{min}\).

# 2.15. Statistical analysis

All experimental results are presented as means \((\mathfrak{n}{=}3)\) \(\pm\) standard deviations (SD). Multiple comparisons were done by analysis of variance (ANOVA), followed by Tukey's range test and independent-sample T-test. Statistical analysis was done by using SPSS 22. The probability value of \(\mathsf{p}<0.05\) was considered significant.

# 3. Results and discussion

# 3.1. Proximate composition

The proximate analysis of wet raw and pretreated CSC is presented in table 1. The moisture content \(74.32\;\%\) was found highest in raw cartilage. However, the fat, ash, protein and carbohydrate contents were \(0.17\,\%\) , \(2.30\%\) , \(12.70\%\) , and \(10.51\;\%\) , respectively.

In the pretreated CSC the moisture content \(81.76\;\%\) was increased significantly and other contents such as fat \(0.10\,\%\) , ash \(0.43\%\) , and carbohydrate \(4.57\%\) were decreased significantly. The protein content \(13.53\;\%\) was observed. The results showed, pretreatment has a significant

