# 3.2. Yield

The appropriate utilization of ultrasound treatment time was facilitated collagen extraction from CSC. The obtained yield of pepsin soluble collagen-II was calculated on a wet, dry and protein weight basis (Figure 2). The yield of ultrasound-treated pepsin soluble collagen-II extracted at treatment times were (UPSCII0) 1.730 g, (UPSCII6) 2.395 g, (UPSCII12) 2.861 g, (UPSCII24) 3.177 g, and (UPSCII36) 3.367 g. The results showed the yield of ultrasound-treated pepsin soluble collagen-II was significantly increased as treatment time increased. The extracted yield was calculated on dry, wet and protein weight basis. The protein weight basis yield was calculated according to the protein contents (\%) of lyophilized ultrasound-treated pepsin soluble collagen-II (UPSCII0: 84.13%, UPSCII6: 81.03%, UPSCII12: 87.86%, UPSCII24: 87.89%, UPSCII36: 87.17%). The protein weight basis yield pepsin soluble collagen-II yield extracted using ultrasound treatment times were higher as compared to the wet and dry weight basis. The pretreated CSC moisture content 81.76\% was used to calculate dry weight basis yield (\%). The results showed, UPSCII36 had the maximum yield on dry, wet, and protein weight basis as compared to other ultrasound-treated collagens UPSCII36> UPSCII24> UPSCII12> UPSCII6> UPSCII0.

The yield of the collagen from CSC were significantly increase because of the ultrasound treatment time and pepsin which might be due to cavitation, disrupting the cell wall effectively and mechanical power promotes enzyme for subsequent extraction. Ultrasound waves were used to release the contents by rupturing the cell walls and was used to activate the immobilized enzymes (Cárcel et al., 2007). These findings are comparable to previously reported yields of
collagen extracted using ultrasonic disruption from jellyfish ranged from 65 to \(70\,\%\) (Khong et al., 2018), soft-shelled turtle collagen which was \(50.75\%\) (Zou et al., 2017), and \(62.5\%\) collagen was extracted with pepsin from the chick sternal cartilage (Cao & Xu, 2008). For the maximum yield of collagen, the key factors were extraction technique, appropriate temperature, and extraction time of reaction mixture. Nevertheless, effective and simpler extraction procedures are preferred for the production of industrial collagen.

# 3.3. UV absorption spectrum

The UV spectra was conducted to measure the concentration and identification of collagen. The maximum absorption of standard pepsin soluble collagen-II at 217.8 to \(218.6\;\mathrm{nm}\), conventional pepsin soluble collagen (PSC-II) at \(216.9\;\mathrm{nm}\) to \(217.9\;\mathrm{nm}\) and ultrasound pepsin soluble collagen (UPSC-II) at \(217.4\;\mathrm{nm}\) to \(218.1\;\mathrm{nm}\) with \(0.1\), \(0.2\;\mathrm{mg/ml}\) concentration were observed (Figure 3). The absorption peak shifted up due to high concentration of collagen. Edwards et al., 1997 suggested that polypeptides chains of collagen have functional groups \(\mathrm{CONH}_{2}\), -COOH, \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) which are the reason for maximum UV absorption. In this current study PSC-II and UPSC-II from CSC did not show absorption at \(280\;\mathrm{nm}\) the reason is the low contents of tyrosin residues/1000 amino acid residues. In the previous study the UV absorption peak did not show at \(280\;\mathrm{nm}\) because of low contents of tyrosin 8.0 to 9.7 residues/1000 amino acid residues (Duan et al., 2009). The previously reported study (Kittiphattanabawon et al., 2010) indicated in collagens no absorption peak at \(280\;\mathrm{nm}\) showed high purity and the evidence for the absences of non-collagenous proteins. The extracted collagen from CSC is collagen-II the UV peaks of PSC-II and UPSC-II are similar to the absorption peaks of standard pepsin soluble collagen-II. The low UV absorption peaks of collagen-II are evidence of the efficient extraction method from CSC.
# 3.4. SDS-PAGE analysis

3.4. SDS-PAGE analysisThe results supported by performing SDS–PAGE analysis which observed no significant changes between all ultrasound treated collagen, control and reference collagen samples in the bands of \(\alpha\) and \(\beta\)-chains in the electrophoretic patterns. Sigma-Aldrich pepsin soluble chicken sternal cartilage collagen type II (Miller) was used as a reference and collagens UPSCII0 (control), UPSCII6, UPSCII12, UPSCII24, and UPSCII36 for the SDS–PAGE electrophoresis analysis, showed \(\alpha\)-chain with a subunit Mr of \(135\,\mathrm{kDa}\) and \(\beta\)- chains in the vicinity of \(245\,\mathrm{kDa}\) (Figure 4). The presence of \(\alpha\) chains combined with the amino acid profile and precipitation properties of NaCl in acidic pH indicates that collagen extracted from CSC was collagen-II. The molecular structure of collagen-II was represented as \([\alpha1\) (II)] 3. Collagen derived from chicken cartilage consists of three identical \(\alpha\)-chains and is categorized as collagen-II (Kambic & McDevitt, 2005). A previous study on collagen extraction from cartilage revealed a structure composed of three duplicate \(\alpha\) chains with Mr of 135-100 kDa (Cao & Xu, 2008) indicating that chicken sternal cartilage is a promising source of collagen-II.

The molecular weight distribution is related to the quality of protein, while the occurrence of \(\alpha\) and \(\beta\) component indicate the purity. No significant difference was found in protein bands of all extracted collagens, as they were identical to the standard collagen band. Cell disruption induced by the dynamic power of ultra-sonication application does not disturb the helical structure of collagen and maintained their integrity (Li et al., 2013). Additionally, the \(\beta\)-chain has relatively low staining strength in all ultrasound treated samples of collagen-II as compared to standard and control collagen-II samples bands due to the pepsin and shear force generated by acoustic cavitation of ultrasound treatment. A longer ultrasound application time with pepsin partially disrupts the \(\beta\) sheet. Therefore, changes were observed in the triple helical structure
following biochemical processing, possibly due to telopeptide breakdown from a region of tropocollagen. The bands of the \(\alpha\)-chain indicate a comparatively stronger staining strength in all the treated samples. Similar findings were obtained from collagen extracted from marine animals in previous reports (Jamilah, Hartina, Hashim, & Sazili, 2013).

# 3.5. Amino acid contents:

Amino acid contents of UPSCII0, UPSCII6, UPSCII12, UPSCII24 and UPSCII36 were 852.58, 866.76, 876.99, 1035.77 and 1018 residues/ 1000 amino acid residues, respectively, from CSC was showed as residues/ 1000 amino acid residues Table 2. In present study, total amino acid content of PSC-II extracted using different ultra-sonication time was found to be in increasing order  UPSCII0 < UPSCII6 < UPSCII12 < UPSCII24 ≥ UPSCII36.

The pepsin soluble collagen-II was rich in, glycine, glutamic acid, alanine, proline, and hydroxyproline. The glycine contents of UPSCII0, UPSCII6, UPSCII12, UPSCII24, and UPSCII36 were 223.62, 281.16, 293.40, 312.87 and 302.88 residues/ 1000 amino acid residues, respectively. The prior studies illustrated glycine amino acid was most abundantly present in all collagen-II (Lin et al., 2006, Nagai et al., 2009, Benjakul et al., 2010).

Amino acid content of proline was increased significantly as the ultrasound treatment time increased 108.96, 115.94, 124.44, 107.37 and 126.44 residues/1000 amino acid residues and hydroxyproline 119.40, 118.31, 117.84, 125.31 and 142.74 residues/1000 amino acid residues, respectively. The proline content was related to the ambient temperature of animal and hydroxyproline contents were varying in different species (Burjanadze et al., 2000). Additionally, proline and hydroxyproline contents are higher than the previously reported, bovine tendon collagen 213 residues/1000 amino acid residues (Nam et al., 2008) and fishbone 174 to 200 /1000 amino acid residues (Liu et al., 2012 & Li et al., 2013). The results showed
amino acid content of ultrasound treated pepsin soluble collagen-II is higher than other collagens extracted by conventional methods. Therefore, the proline and hydroxyproline amino acid contents are significantly higher and may have potential for better thermal stability and structural integrity of collagens.

Moreover, methionine, histidine and tyrosine contents were found to be very low in all extracted ultrasound-treated collagens. The high amino acid contents of collagen and removal of non-collagenous protein were because of the removal of telopeptides using the pepsin enzyme digestion and potential application of ultrasound. The ultrasound time had physical and chemical effects on the conservation and modification of extracted pepsin soluble collagen-II from CSC. The physicochemical characteristics of collagens were influenced directly due to the amino acid profile and cross-linkage abilities (Lin et al., 2006).

# 3.6. Zeta potential and Isoelectric point (PI)

Zeta potential analysis was used to determine the surface charge state of protein particles due to the ionization of amino acid residue (Zhang et al., 2019). The pepsin soluble collagen-II was extracted with acid from chicken sternal cartilage (CSC) found to be acidic. The isoelectric point (PI) of the samples were determined at the pH value where the zeta potential was zero (Abedin et al., 2013). The (Figure 5) demonstrate the PI of UPSCII0, UPSCII6, UPSCII12, UPSCII24 and UPSCII36 were 5.51, 5.57, 5.74, 5.87 and 5.64, respectively. Generally, the PI values of collagens are 6 to 9 (Foegeding et al., 1996). No significant difference was observed in the PI values of non-ultrasound treated collagen-II (UPSCII0) and ultrasound-treated collagen-II on the same pH. A little increase in PI was observed as the ultrasound time was increased. The results showed in ultrasound-treated pepsin soluble collagen-II no significance difference was also found in the zeta potential values and the chargeability under the same pH. Therefore, it can be
conclude that the disruption of the small fragments of collagen surface leads to reduce the size without inducing any change in native state by the shear forces of cavitation induced by the ultrasound treatment time. The ultrasound application were entrapped coordinate bond, forming a soluble complex with improved stability.

# 3.7. Secondary structure of collagen-II

# 3.7.1. Circular dichroism (CD)

The circular dichroism (CD) spectroscopy provides the information of protein secondary structure. In this study, CD spectra of ultrasound-treated collagens UPSCII0, UPSCII6, UPSCII12, UPSCII24, and UPSCII36 were exhibited rotatory maximum (positive band) at 221.69 nm, 221.65 nm, 221.77 nm, 221.89 nm, and 221.89 nm, minimum (negative band) at 198.32 nm, 198.96 nm, 199.08 nm, 204.05 nm, and 209.73 nm, and cross point (zero rotation) at around 214.74 nm, respectively (Figure 6). The results revealed the confirmation of triple helical structure of protein extracted from CSC, have typical characteristics of collagen-II.

The present study indicates the triple-helical structure of ultrasound-treated pepsin soluble collagen-II from CSC was not completely destroyed due to an enzyme (pepsin) and different ultrasound treatment times. According to the previous studies collagen CD spectra showed the positive and negative bands that were observed at 197 nm and 199 nm from adult black drum, at 221 nm and 198 nm was observed from chicken cartilage, and at 230 nm and 204 nm was observed from eel skin collagen (Cao & Xu, 2008; Veeruraj, Arumugam, & Balasubramanian, 2013).

However, the ultrasound-treated PSC-II exhibited high amino acid contents, its triple helical structure is more stable due to intra and inter-molecular cross-linkages. The CD spectra of collagen extracted with pepsin and different ultrasound treatment times UPSCII0, UPSCII6,
UPSCII12 interpreted the triple helical structure is stable but partial denaturation of the triple helical structure of UPSCII24, and UPSCII36 due to pepsin digestion and longer ultra-sonication time.

# 3.7.2. Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy

The Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra of collagen-II, main absorption peaks were amide A, amide B, amide I, amide II, and amide III from CSC (Figure 7). The amide A absorption peak of UPSCII0, UPSCII6, UPSCII12, UPSCII24, and UPSCII36 were found at 3307.71 cm-1, 3307.93 cm-1, 3301.71 cm-1, 3300.93 cm-1, and 3311.04 cm-1, respectively. The results showed the peaks were slightly shifted to lower peak numbers as compared to other proteins range from 3400-3440 cm\(^{-1}\). The main factor to shift the wave number was hydrogen bonds formed between the N-H group and functional groups (Muyonga, Cole, & Duodu, 2004). Amide B peaks position of UPSCII0, UPSCII6, UPSCII12, UPSCII24, and UPSCII36 were shifted lower wavenumber 2923.79 cm-1, 2933.90 cm-1, 2921.46 cm-1, 2937.90 cm-1 and 2927.68 cm-1, respectively. These peaks present the asymmetrical stretch of \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\) and C-H (Hsu, Weng, Liao, & Chen, 2005; Payne & Veis, 1988). The amide A and amide B absorption peaks were high because the increase in protein and protein intermolecular cross linkages through hydrogen bonds of molecular weight of collagens.

Amide I peaks of UPSCII0,UPSCII6, UPSCII12, UPSCII24, and UPSCII36 were found at 1633.74 cm-1, 1629.86 cm-1, 1636.85 cm-1, 1633.96 cm-1, and 1637.63 cm-1, respectively. Amide I peak vibration mode was mainly due to \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) stretching coupled with N-H bond. Payne & Veis (1988) reported, amide I peak shift to the lower wave number that was linked with decrease in the molecular order. The results suggested due to enzymatic extraction the
telopeptides of PSC-II were partially removed, might be loss reactive amino acid such as lysine, hydroxylysine, and histidine for crosslinking at telopeptide region.

Amide II peaks of UPSCII0, UPSCII6, UPSCII12, UPSCII24, and UPSCII36 were displayed at \(1550.54\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), \(1546.65\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), \(1543.54\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), \(1549.76\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), and \(1547.43\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), respectively, the N-H bend coupled C-N stretching vibration. The amide III peaks of UPSCII0, UPSCII6, UPSCII12, UPSCII24, and UPSCII36 were observed at \(1452.56\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), \(1452.56\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), \(1452.56\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), \(1453.56\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), and \(1456.45\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), respectively presents the triple helical structure of collagens. The slight difference in the peak absorption of collagens were due to ultra-sonication time and pepsin digestion during the extraction. The resulting ultrasound pepsin soluble collagen-II from CSC might be partially altered the secondary structure, when pepsin breakdown the telopeptide region of tropocollagen. The peaks of collagens assignment for the region between \(1200\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(1400\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) were showed \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\) bend at \(1396.57\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), CO-O-C asymmetric stretch; glycogen and nucleic acid, twisting at \(1334.37\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), CN stretching and NH deformation at \(1236.39\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) (Bachmann, Gomes & Zezell, 2005; Cao & Xu, 2008). The FTIR spectra of the present study suggesting the triple-helical structure was well stable in non-ultrasound and ultrasound-treated pepsin soluble collagen-II from CSC.

# 3.8. Thermal stability

The differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) of PSC-II from CSC showed two endothermic peaks, which indicates the thermal denaturation \(\left(\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{d}}\right)\) and melting temperature \(\left(\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{m}}\right)\) by heating (Figure 8). In this study, DSC spectra of PSC-II extracted with ultrasound treatment time, Tm of UPSCII0, UPSCII6, UPSCII12, UPSCII24, and UPSCII36 was \(50.91^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), \(55.90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), \(57.54^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), \(59.17^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), and \(54.18^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), and the values of \(\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{d}}\) were observed at \(44.83^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), \(46.99^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), \(52.21^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), \(53.51^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(44.97^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), respectively. The ultrasound-treated collagen-II have higher
thermal stability as compared to non-ultrasound-treated, \( \mathrm{T_{m}} \) significantly increased as the time of ultrasound treatment was increased. The results suggested the difference in thermal stability of collagen correlated with the physicochemical conversion using ultrasound treatment time during extraction and because of hydration. The covalent cross linkages was partially altered by high ultrasound treatment time (36 min), so the \( \mathrm{T_{m}} \) of UPSCII36 was observed \( 54.18^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) less than \( \mathrm{T_{m}} \) of UPSCII24 was \( 59.17^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \).

In the present study, thermal denaturation \( \left(\mathrm{T_{d}}\right) \) was observed higher in the land animal collagen extracted using ultra-sonication as compare to many aquatic animal collagens. In the previous studies, the \( \mathrm{T_{d}} \) of collagen from different sources such as, calf collagen, brown-banded bamboo shark collagen, eel fish skin collagen, porcine skin collagen, Silvertip shark collagen, whale shark collagen, chicken articular collagen and chicken cartilage collagens were observed \( 40.8^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), \( 36.73^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), \( 35.0^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), \( 37^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), \( 30.00^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), \( 31^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), \( 47^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) and \( 43.8^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), respectively (Jeevithan et al., 2015; Elango et al., 2015; Pearce et al., 1978; Muyonga et al., 2004; Helen et al., 2005; Woo et al., 2008 & Veeruraj et al., 2013). In the prior study (Zou et al., 2017) collagen was extracted from soft shelled turtle calipash using conventional and ultrasound method the Td was observed \( 43.98^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) and \( 35.19^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), respectively. The ultrasound-treated collagen displayed higher thermal stability and the animal collagen showed higher Td values than collagen from aquatic sources. Generally, it was associated with body and environmental temperature of their habitat (Muyonga, Cole, & Duodu, 2004)

Amino acid contents were responsible for the structure, inter-chain hydrogen bonding of triple helix and thermal denaturation of protein. The Td values were associated with the amino acid profile and were justified with amount of hydroxyproline and proline (Okazaki & Osako, 2014). Additionally, the Td values also indicates unfolding of helix structure of the protein to the
random coil that changes the physicochemical characteristics of protein (Elango et al., 2015). Thermal stability of ultrasound-treated pepsin soluble collagen-II would have the potential for the production of biomedical, nutraceuticals, cosmetics and biomaterials product.

# 3.9. Microscopic structure

The pepsin soluble collagen-II extracted in the present study was looked like soft white cotton, sheet-like a sponge with loose fibers observed by the naked eye. The microscopic structure of ultrasound-treated pepsin soluble collagen-II from CSC was observed by using scanning electron microscopy (SEM) (Figure 9A, B, C, D and E). The non-ultrasound-treated collagen (UPSCII0) showed a multilayered aggregated structure, less fibril dominated network, have less number of fiber like filaments and disruption of non-helical fibril due to the pepsin enzyme. The course surface was related to the dehydration which indicated the condensation of polymer (Oechsle et al., 2015).

However, the ultrasound-treated UPSCII6, UPSCII12, UPSCII24, and UPSCII36 were observed porous, loose and large number of cross-sections with an inter-connected fibril network pore configuration. As ultra-sonication treatment time increased these pores and fibril-like structure networks of collagens were observed much defined. In UPSCII36 extended ultrasound treatment time affected the length and structure of the fibrils. The microscopic structure of marine collagen extracted using ultrasound was observed loose, porous and homogenous as compared to conventional method Zou et al., (2017).

The changes in microscopic structure are more related to the functional properties of the collagen. The microscopic structure of ultrasound-treated collagen-II indicated the more porous, loose patterns with long fibril network caused by the mechanical oscillations and cavitation of the ultrasonic waves. The present study concluded that the ultra-sonication treatment time might
be induced apparent physical variations in the microscopic structure of pepsin soluble collagen-II from CSC. These structural variations can be further related to the functional properties.

# 3.10. Functional characterization of collagen-II

# 3.10.1. Water holding capacity

Water holding capacity (WHC) is the ability of proteins to prevent water from being released or expelled from their three-dimensional structure (Zayas, 1997). Most important functional properties of proteins are related to their interaction with water, therefore the selection of proteins with an appropriate WHC is vital in food development. Many factors influence its development, the lowest WHC is usually recorded at the isoelectric point (PI) where the net charge of the protein of a solvent is zero and protein-protein interactions are dominant.

In this study, WHC of UPSCII0, UPSCII6, UPSCII12, UPSCII24, and UPSCII36 were observed \(562.19\%\) , \(566.11\%\) , \(576.29\%\) , \(595.11\%\) , and \(572.23\%\) , respectively. The ultrasound treatment was exhibited significant increase in WHC of ultrasound-treated collagen-II. The WHC of distilled water \(539.07\%\) was significantly lower than the glycerol \(623.90\;\%\) Table 3. The WHC can fluctuate due to different factors including ionic strength, protein concentration and binding ability (Bouafsoun et al., 2007). The high hydrophilic groups were formed hydrogen bonds with water and exhibited a good ability to absorb and retain water due to the ultrasound application (Hou et al., 2012; Pearce & Kinsella, 1978).

Similar results were reported in the previous study (Zou et al., 2017) WHC of ultrasound-treated collagen from turtle calipash was improved by \(75.3\%\) compared to the collagen extracted by conventional method (control). In the present study, the ability to hold water the surface hydrophobicity and charged groups of collagen was increased due to ultrasound treatment time because of the shear forces of cavitation showed good moisture absorption and retention
property. High-intensity ultrasound has been found as an effective food processing technology that widely use in the food industry. Ultrasound pepsin soluble collagen-II have beneficial capacity for application in the pharmaceutical, food and cosmetic industries.

# 3.10.2. Water absorption capacity

The water-absorption capacity (WAC) is expressed as the amount of water absorbed per gram of (protein) collagen powder. This property is based on the direct interaction of molecules with water and other solutes. WAC of ultrasound treated pepsin soluble collagen-II at different time UPSCII0, UPSCII6, UPSCII12, UPSCII24, and UPSCII36 were \(101.39\%\) , \(124.06\%\) , \(144.77\%\) , \(150.34\%\) , and \(130.90\%\) , respectively Table 3. The results showed the WAC increase significantly as the ultrasound treatment time increased. But the longer time of ultrasound application UPSCII36 showed less WAC as compared to other ultrasound treated collagen samples. In the ultrasound processing shear forces of the acoustic cavitation, power density, temperature, processing time have the potential for optimizing this technique for industrial applications. The ultrasound treated collagen-II from CSC will be a suitable ingredient where hydration is desirable parameter in the products.

# 3.10.3. Oil absorption capacity

The functional properties of proteins are involve the proteins-lipids interactions, formation of emulsions, fat emulsification and flavor absorption. Oil absorption of protein is affected by protein source, processing conditions, composition and temperature. The OAC of pepsin soluble collagen-II of non-ultrasound-treated UPSCII0: \(172.93\%\) and ultrasound-treated were UPSCII6: \(177.33\%\) , UPSCII12: \(191.47\%\) , UPSCII24: \(194.93\%\) and UPSCII36: \(130.90\%\) as the results exhibited Table 3. It could be possible due to the various factors such as source (raw material), protein type, a degree of hydrolysis of proline, processing method, non-polar side
chains and the type of oil used (Kristinsson & Rasco, 2000). The results showed OAC of ultrasound treated pepsin soluble collagen-II will be considered as an important property for the different product ingredients, which was related to the flavor and texture retention of products of meat and confectionary industries (Bougatef et al., 2007).

# 3.10.4. Foaming property

The foaming ability (FA) and foaming stability (FS) are functional properties of protein. The FA of ultrasound treated pepsin soluble collagen-II at pH 4, 7 and 10 of UPSCII0: \(139.7\%\) , \(130.7\%\) , \(137.8\;\%\) ; UPSCII6: \(149.3\%\) , \(135.7\%\) , \(151.0\%\) ; UPSCII12: \(160.8\%\) , \(151.3\%\) , \(166.5\%\) ; UPSCII24: \(169.4\%\) , \(158.6\%\) , \(173.1\%\) and UPSCII36: \(150.0\%\) , \(130.5\%\) , \(144.5\%\) , respectively. The results demonstrated FA was significantly high \((\mathsf{p}<0.05)\) at \(\mathrm{pH}\,10\) and \(\mathrm{pH}\,4\) as compared to \(\mathrm{pH}\,7\) , which was greatly influenced by ultrasound treatment and pH (Figure 10A and B).

The FS was also affected due to the pH and different ultra-sonication treatment time. The FS of ultrasound treated pepsin soluble collagen-II at pH 4, 7 and 10 of UPSCII0: \(126.6\%\) , \(124.0\%\) , \(121.1\;\%\) ; UPSCII6: \(132.9\%\) , \(113.9\%\) , \(142.7\%\) ; UPSCII12: \(137.5\%\) , \(129.9\%\) , \(142.7\%\) ; UPSCII24: \(154.9\%\) , \(128.7\%\) , \(153.9\%\) and UPSCII36: \(135.9\%\) , \(116.9\%\) , \(129.5\%\) , respectively. The UPSCII24 showed significantly high foaming stability at \(\mathrm{pH}\,4\) , and 10 as compared to the UPSCII0, UPSCII6, UPSCII12, and UPSCII36 (Figure 10A).

The results revealed that FA and FS of pepsin soluble collagen-II from CSC were significantly \((\mathsf{P}<0.05)\) affected by pH and ultrasound treatment time. The ultrasound application has a significant effect, as the treatment time increased FA and FS values increased significantly due to increase in charge density, unfolding and rearranging of protein at the interface encouraged by ultra-sonication (Hunter, Pugh, Franks, & Jameson, 2008). Protein unfolding,
change in the formation of interfacial protein was exhibited hydrophobic associate in alkali solution induced air-water interface, which enhanced encapsulation of air bubbles. The effect of an ultrasound treatment on liquid are mainly because of cavitation induced by ultrasound waves, cavitation induced physical forces. The interactions of protein and water is important for the formation of foam and its stability. Ultrasound pepsin soluble collagen-II from CSC can be useful in the food system as a foaming agent.

# 3.10.5. Emulsifying property

The emulsifying activity index (EAI) and emulsifying stability index (ESI) of pepsin soluble collagen-II from CSC were significantly effected \((\mathsf{p}<0.05)\) by both ultrasound treatment and pH. The EAI represents the ability of proteins to be absorbed in the interface of fat globules and the aqueous phase during the formation of emulsion. The emulsification properties was changed by fluctuating ultrasound cavitation intensity and effect of pH can be considered as an important factor for protein functionality. To compare EAI of non-ultrasound-treated at different pH 4, 7 and 10 UPSCII0: 32.71 m\(^2\)/g, 25.62 m\(^2\)/g, 43.51 m\(^2\)/g with ultrasound-treated UPSCII6: 36.52 m\(^2\)/g, 32.53 m\(^2\)/g, 53.25 m\(^2\)/g; UPSCII12: 48.92 m\(^2\)/g, 42.17 m\(^2\)/g, 65.29 m\(^2\)/g; UPSCII24: 61.04 m\(^2\)/g, 51.92 m\(^2\)/g, 71.80 m\(^2\)/g; UPSCII36: 46.48 m\(^2\)/g, 41.20 m\(^2\)/g, 62.90 m\(^2\)/g, respectively (Figure 11A, and B). The EAI increased as ultrasound treatment time increased at the pH 4 and 10 as compared to the pH 7. Most of the proteins are less soluble at their PI and their emulsifying activity is minimal due to change in solubility and the interface (Akram & Zhang, 2020). The results showed, proteins have the potential to be adsorbed in the aqueous and the oil phase (Pearce and Kinsella, 1978). The ultrasound-treated pepsin soluble collagen-II showed a high surface hydrophobicity that enhances emulsifying activity and increased molecular flexibility and surface hydrophobicity.
The ESI was the estimation of protein capacity for staying at water into oil interface after emulsion storage (Mohanty et al., 1988). The ESI of collagen that specifies the resistance caused by the changes in the pH and physicochemical properties by ultrasound treatment. The ESI values at different pH 4, 7, and 10 of UPSCII0: 37.60 min, 22.25 min and 44.40 min were compared with UPSCII6: 43.98 min, 35.36 min and 53.16 min; UPSCII12: 50.10 min, 43.62 min and 68.75 min; UPSCII24: 64.77 min, 44.97 min and 74.01 min and UPSCII36: 51.27 min, 33.25 min and 55.09 min, respectively. The results compared favourably with the emulsifying properties of the collagen extracted from marine sources at different pH 4, 7 and 10 (Huang, Kuo, Wu, & Tsai, 2016). The results illustrated the ESI of ultrasound treated collagens were higher than the non-ultrasound treated collagen at pH 4 and 10 as compared to the pH 7 and decreased as ultrasound time was extended, which may change their lipophilic-hydrophilic arrangements, molecular size and length. The ultrasound application had a significant effect on emulsifying properties that confirming pepsin soluble collagen-II from CSC will be used as an emulsifying agent in industrial process.

# 4. Conclusion

The PSC-II extraction was performed efficiently using the ultrasound treatment time from poultry by-product chicken sternal cartilage (CSC). As the ultrasound treatment time increased, the yield of PSC-II increased significantly \((\mathsf{p}<0.05)\) and the highest yield was obtained at 36 min of ultra-sonication. The ultrasound-treated enzymatic collagen was identified as collagen-II with higher amino acid contents. The thermal stability gradually increased as the ultra-sonication treatment time increased, the microscopic structure of ultrasound-treated PSC-II indicated the more porous by the mechanical oscillations and cavitation of the ultrasonic waves. Additionally, ultra-sonication treatment significantly improve the physicochemical and functional properties of
